## Comprehensive Logging for Webhooks

### Setup Logging

Implement dual console/file logging for webhook testing:

```typescript
// Set up logging
const LOG_DIR = path.join(process.cwd(), '..', '..', 'logs');
if (!fs.existsSync(LOG_DIR)) {
  fs.mkdirSync(LOG_DIR, { recursive: true });
}
const TIMESTAMP = new Date().toISOString().replace(/:/g, '-').replace(/\..+/, '');
const LOG_FILE = path.join(LOG_DIR, `webhook_test_${TIMESTAMP}.log`);

// Logger function that writes to console and file
function log(message: string) {
  console.log(message);
  fs.appendFileSync(LOG_FILE, message + '\n');
}
```

### Diagnostic Logging

Log diagnostic information to help with troubleshooting:

```typescript
// Detailed error logging
try {
  // API call
} catch (error) {
  log(`Error: ${error.message}`);
  
  // Log detailed error info to file only
  fs.appendFileSync(LOG_FILE, `\nDetailed error:\n${JSON.stringify(error, null, 2)}\n`);
  
  // Provide helpful feedback
  if (error.code === 'ECONNREFUSED') {
    log('  → Connection refused. Make sure the server is running.');
  }
}
```

## Common Pitfalls and Solutions

### Ngrok Not Starting Correctly

If ngrok doesn't start correctly:
- Check if another instance is already running (`ps aux | grep ngrok`)
- Verify port 4040 is available for the ngrok admin interface
- Check ngrok authentication status (`ngrok authtoken`)

### Webhook Signature Verification Failures

If production webhooks fail with signature verification errors:
- Verify the webhook secret matches between your server and the provider dashboard
- Check for clock drift if timestamps are used in signatures
- Ensure the raw request body is used for verification (not parsed JSON)

### Connection Issues

If you can't connect to ngrok URLs:
- Verify the tunnel is active in the ngrok dashboard (http://localhost:4040)
- Check if your firewall is blocking outgoing connections
- Try a different ngrok region if international connectivity is an issue

